::ENG3_U04_PRE_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: A deserted library still has names written on the checkout cards. Source note: Libraries in literature can represent shared memory and public learning. Which research question best fits the source set? {
~What color are the checkout cards?#This is too narrow and not interpretive.
=How do the checkout cards help develop the idea of shared public memory?#Correct. This is an interpretive research question supported by both sources.
~Who designed the library building?#This asks for outside factual information not supplied.
~Are libraries better than schools?#This adds an unsupported comparison.
}
::ENG3_U04_PRE_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: A lamp burns in a closed mill office. Which claim is strongest? {
~The office has a lamp.#This is summary only.
~The mill is closed.#This is a detail, not a claim about meaning.
=The lamp symbolizes uncertain hope inside a place associated with loss.#Correct. This is a defensible literary analysis claim.
~The lamp should be turned off.#This is practical advice.
}
::ENG3_U04_PRE_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: An article begins with a broken bridge, gives examples of neighbors helping each other cross, and ends by claiming that cooperation repairs communities. Which analysis best explains organization? {
~The article has a broken bridge.#This is summary.
~The article is about neighbors and bridges.#This names topic but does not analyze structure.
~The bridge should be fixed by the city.#This is not text analysis.
=The article moves from problem to examples to claim, showing how cooperation answers a shared need.#Correct. This analyzes organization and meaning.
}
::ENG3_U04_PRE_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source note: Public objects in literature can preserve community history when characters notice traces of repeated use. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=Objects used by many people can suggest a community's past when characters observe signs of use.#Correct. This accurately restates the source.
~Public objects are always more important than private objects.#This adds an unsupported comparison.
~Characters must explain every object directly.#This is not stated.
~Community history only appears in old buildings.#This is too narrow.
}
::ENG3_U04_PRE_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Draft: The author uses structure good because it starts with a problem and then examples. Which revision is best? {
~The author structures good with a problem and examples.#This is not standard academic English.
~The author has a good structure and examples.#This is vague.
=The author uses a problem-to-example structure to clarify how the issue affects different people.#Correct. This is clearer and more academic.
~The structure is nice because examples are there.#This remains vague.
}
::ENG3_U04_PRE_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The source explains monuments preserve memory the poem shows names carved into stone. Which revision follows standard English conventions? {
~The source explains monuments preserve memory the poem shows names carved into stone.#This is a run-on.
=The source explains that monuments preserve memory, and the poem shows names carved into stone.#Correct. This is a complete, correctly punctuated sentence.
~The source explaining monuments preserve memory and the poem showing names.#This is a fragment.
~Because monuments preserve memory and names carved into stone.#This is incomplete.
}
::ENG3_U04_PRE_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: A locked gate holds roses just beyond the bars. Source note: Gates can symbolize boundaries, and flowers can suggest desire. Which analysis is strongest? {
~The gate has roses beyond it.#This is summary.
~The gate is locked because it has bars.#This is literal.
~The source says gates and flowers can be symbols.#This repeats the source only.
=The locked gate and roses create a contrast between restriction and desire.#Correct. This explains how the elements create meaning.
}
::ENG3_U04_PRE_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Scenario: A student writes complex text analysis with slang and personal reactions. What revision is most needed? {
~Add more personal reactions.#This would not improve academic tone.
~Remove all evidence.#Evidence is needed.
=Use a formal academic tone and explain how the text works.#Correct. Complex text analysis requires appropriate tone and voice.
~Change the topic completely.#The issue is tone, not topic.
}
::ENG3_U04_PRE_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: A village well is sealed, but people still gather beside it. Source note: Shared rituals can preserve community after an object's original purpose changes. Which synthesis is strongest? {
~The well is sealed and people gather.#This is summary only.
~The source is about rituals.#This uses only the source.
=The sealed well still represents community because the passage shows continued gathering and the source explains how rituals preserve connection.#Correct. This synthesizes both sources.
~The well should be reopened.#This is practical advice.
}
::ENG3_U04_PRE_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Text summary: A report uses headings for each library service before arguing that libraries are civic infrastructure. Which sentence shows subject understanding? {
~The report is about libraries doing stuff.#This is vague and informal.
~The headings are in the report.#This is summary only.
~Libraries have books and rooms.#This is too limited.
=The report presents public libraries as civic infrastructure by explaining several services they provide.#Correct. This accurately explains the subject.
}
